1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,925
Another internationalization library?
It's a fair question, with a simple answer:

2
00:00:05,925 --> 00:00:11,176
translation belongs where you already work.
In your JSX, in Vite, in VS Code.

3
00:00:11,176 --> 00:00:14,655
It's your own ecosystem
that should be helping you.

4
00:00:16,467 --> 00:00:18,996
That's why viteTranslate
lives in all three.

5
00:00:18,996 --> 00:00:21,296
Write the sentence, get the translation.

6
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:26,441
Write the sentence in JSX,
just as it reads. The key?

7
00:00:26,441 --> 00:00:29,564
viteTranslate makes it: nothing to manage.

8
00:00:31,467 --> 00:00:32,631
And here's VS Code.

9
00:00:32,631 --> 00:00:36,125
An official extension brings
translation into the editor.

10
00:00:36,125 --> 00:00:39,865
Every sentence with its status,
highlighted the way you like.

11
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:44,057
Not in French yet?

12
00:00:44,057 --> 00:00:49,925
You get the English, with a marker only
developers see: it never reaches production.

13
00:00:49,925 --> 00:00:51,951
Never a blank, never a crash.

14
00:00:53,333 --> 00:00:54,387
Time for a build.

15
00:00:54,387 --> 00:00:58,604
The Vite plugin brings every language
file back in sync, on its own:

16
00:00:58,604 --> 00:01:02,882
extraction is part of Vite's lifecycle,
no extra command to remember.

17
00:01:02,882 --> 00:01:06,913
And French gets a chunk of its own,
loaded only when it's needed.

18
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:10,616
And in production, it travels light.

19
00:01:10,616 --> 00:01:13,832
Zero runtime dependencies,
five kilobytes gzipped.

20
00:01:13,832 --> 00:01:18,977
ICU plurals and dates come precompiled,
so the browser gets values ready to use.

21
00:01:20,167 --> 00:01:23,717
The panel already knows what's
waiting for a translation.

22
00:01:23,717 --> 00:01:27,953
Click the new one, then French,
and the file opens right on the key.

23
00:01:27,953 --> 00:01:30,382
Type, save, and there it is, in French.

24
00:01:34,767 --> 00:01:35,934
A thousand keys?

25
00:01:35,934 --> 00:01:40,310
viteTranslate works with an LLM too,
whichever one you like.

26
00:01:40,310 --> 00:01:45,050
It shows the cost, asks before spending,
and checks every result.

27
00:01:46,133 --> 00:01:48,377
Sometimes, simple is better.

28
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:56,503
Voilà!

29
00:02:01,833 --> 00:02:05,280
Want to see it in action?
The site has three live pages.

30
00:02:06,567 --> 00:02:10,720
The Playground: every feature,
live, right next to its code.

31
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:15,923
Edge cases: one row per case,
mistakes included.

32
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:21,737
And a whole restaurant:
two hundred and twenty-eight sentences,

33
00:02:21,737 --> 00:02:24,232
four languages, translated by the LLM.

34
00:02:27,433 --> 00:02:31,139
Lingui, FormatJS and i18next
are great libraries.

35
00:02:31,139 --> 00:02:34,163
The difference isn't any single feature:

36
00:02:34,163 --> 00:02:37,793
it's having all of them,
built to work together.

37
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:45,878
Ten features, one package: in your JSX,
in VS Code, in Vite, and in production.

38
00:02:45,878 --> 00:02:49,107
All in one place, right where you write.

39
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,572
viteTranslate. On npm,
in the VS Code Marketplace, and on GitHub.

40
00:02:55,572 --> 00:02:59,230
You write the sentence:
viteTranslate does the rest.

41
00:03:00,533 --> 00:03:03,544
Oh, I almost forgot:
it's free and open source.

42
00:03:03,544 --> 00:03:05,850
Buy my author a coffee, if you like!
